# Fix similar findings together

When a problem is pointed out, do not fix only the cited spot and stop. Before fixing, check whether the same kind of gap exists elsewhere.

- Look in the same file, in callees with the same contract, and in the paired code: the series path and the scatter path, the small-data path and the large-data path (§5.5), the pyramid build and the streaming append that extends it, the D3D11 backend and the GPU backends after it, the WPF host and the hosts after it, the JIT and NativeAOT builds.
- Examples: an off-by-one at a chunk boundary in the min/max pyramid build and the same in the M4 readout; a texture view released before a resize on the swap-chain path and not on the shared-texture path of the WPF host; a `[DoesNotReturn]` throw helper used in one hot method while its sibling throws inline. PR #288 made the WPF view poll until the newest read-back copy is read and left the shared-texture path taking an older copy; PR #294's WPF tests found it there.
- If you find the same kind of problem, fix it together with the cited spot.
- When the fix changes what a helper is given (a range instead of the whole column), search the callers of the helper's siblings too: helpers that do the same work for another caller carry the same defect. PR #465 made `ElementPixelsInto` map only a part's run of rows for points and rects, and left `ElementPixels`, which maps the whole column for segments and text; the review's "Not checked" pointed at it.
- If you find none, say what you checked and that nothing remains.
- A new path over data an older path already handles takes the older path's conditions with it. PR #342's hit test took a facet's gathered rows back to the plot's table for every layer, an image's element positions too; the drawing path (`TakeNodes`) already left the layers without rows (`FacetData.Rowless`) out, and the review found the difference.
- A member added beside an existing virtual one (a color beside a hit test) is overridden by every class that overrides the existing one: list them with a search for the existing member's overrides, and write a PR body's "every" from that list. PR #352 added `HitColor` to `SplitPlan` alone; `StatPlan` and `StackedSeriesPlan`, which delegate `Hit` to their parts, kept the layer's one color, and the PR body called that the series color.
- A value whose meaning widens (a kind that becomes a set of flags, a place that is now two places at once) is looked for at every reader by the value's members on the right side of the comparison (`rg '== HitAreaKind\.|!= HitAreaKind\.|is HitAreaKind\.|is not HitAreaKind\.|case HitAreaKind\.'`), not by the name of the variable that holds it. PR #387's search was `Kind == HitAreaKind`, and `SelectionTool` held the kind in a local `area`; the review found it, and the Shift drag did not select over an annotation.
- A special case written for one kind of a family (a time scale's Δx written in seconds) is a question to every other kind: go through the family's list (the scale specs: linear, log, time, band) and say for each what the general path does with the value. PR #395 wrote a time axis's Δx in seconds and left a band axis's to `TickAxis.WriteValue`, which writes a position as its category's name: a difference of 2 came out as "c2", and the review found it.

```text
# BAD — fixes the NaN skip in the min/max build of the pyramid; the reply says "fixed"
# GOOD — fixes it there, in the append path, and in the density aggregation; the reply names the three and the search
```
